# width: 120
# The standard profile's which-key popup IS its `Ctrl+K` chords. A
# `Ctrl+K S` typed fast, the same `S` after a pause past the chord
# timeout, and `S` in the popup the palette's `whichkey.leader` opens all
# run ONE command — VS Code's Save All (its `Ctrl+K S` on Windows; Linux
# and macOS have Save without Formatting there, which mnml does not
# have). It used to be three things: the fast chord and the pause ran
# `view.activity_sessions`, and the palette's popup was the vim leader
# tree titled `Ctrl+K`, where `s` descended into `+split`. The sessions
# section moved to `Ctrl+K A`.
write a.txt "AAA\n"
write b.txt "BBB\n"
open a.txt
type 1
open b.txt
type 1
expect dirty true

# Fast.
key ctrl+k
key s
wait 300
expect file a.txt contains "1AAA"
expect file b.txt contains "1BBB"
expect dirty false

# The popup from the palette: its `s` row is the same command.
type 2
open a.txt
type 2
command whichkey.leader
expect screen contains "┌ Ctrl+K "
expect screen contains "s → save all"
expect screen lacks "s → +split"
type s
wait 300
expect screen lacks "┌ Ctrl+K "
expect file a.txt contains "12AAA"
expect file b.txt contains "12BBB"

# A pause after Ctrl+K: the same popup, the same command.
type 3
key ctrl+k
wait 700
expect screen contains "s → save all"
type s
wait 300
expect file a.txt contains "123AAA"
expect dirty false

# The sessions section kept a chord, VS Code's free `Ctrl+K A`.
key ctrl+k
key a
wait 300
expect screen contains "SESSIONS"
